Technical level: Intermediate+.  Some working knowledge of the \ul Text Editor and\ulnone\par
\ul Corrector\ulnone  (\i TECO)\i0  utility program (as implemented on many computer systems including the \i DEC PDP-8\i0 ) is required; \i TECOC\i0  is a TECO implementation for \i MS-DOS\super tm\nosupersub\i0  and \i Windows\super tm\nosupersub\i0  which is available for free download on the Internet.  Access to a TECOC reference document is highly recommended to obtain a full understanding of the more arcane TECO commands used.  It is hoped the reader gains insight into the inner workings of this program merely by reading this document.\par
\par
Note: Advanced TECO users may use a larger subset of TECOC in their manual editing work; however, some of the commands described in this document will exceed even \b their\b0  working knowledge of TECO.  Certain commands are hardly ever used outside of TECO macros; specific commands are virtually unique to TECOC.\par
\par
\b Introduction.\b0\par
\par
Various components of the \ul\i P?S Extended PDP-8 Simulator\ulnone\i0  (\i PEPS\i0 ) package (an extension\par
to the \i SIMH\i0  PDP-8 simulator) for Windows\super tm\nosupersub  use the \i STRIP.TEC\i0  macro program to filter and correct various file quirks in output files created within the SIMH simulated PDP-8 environment.  These aberrations are usually caused by minor programming errors that went virtually unnoticed when originally used on then-present day hardware.  This was often because physical lineprinters and similar output devices generally could tolerate modest formatting errors (and many programmers were either complacent or novices with regard to proper programming for lineprinters).\par
\par
Prior to the use of simulators, printer users were willing to tear off output\par
perhaps after manually separating pages (or at least removing extraneous pages on\par
either side of the listing).  Few programmers cared about minor anomalies; merely\par
obtaining a listing was often considered a luxury.\par
\par
Note: Some users were especially picky regarding the way fan-folded listings were folded, preferring a top page with the fold at the bottom; as necessary, this could include an additional blank page after the last printed page with the fold at the top.  Failing to obtain this precise result led to manually refolding the pages.\par
Savvy users learned the behavior of specific lineprinters to generally assure the final result would be obtained without resorting to refolding; however, if the only "problem" was the "wrong" fold direction, listings were generally manually refolded while waiting for the next file to print.  Access to lineprinters was often limited such that elaborate scenarios were planned to obtain printed output (sometimes from relatively remote computer installations).\par
\par
\b Processing output files.\b0\par
\par
In "modern times" we expect to either read the lineprinter-oriented output directly in an editor such as Windows\super tm\nosupersub  Notepad\super tm\nosupersub , or perhaps publish the output through a word-processor or a .PDF file creator.  As such, all minor aberrations must first be removed for the benefit of these "fragile" utilities, which tend to easily reveal such quirks (which are often dealt with in a heavy-handed manner).\par
\par
The burden of printing has shifted to post-processing within the host environment\par
(in this instance Windows\super tm\nosupersub  or perhaps MS-DOS\super tm\nosupersub ) which can be somewhat "hostile" to the raw output created by prevailing programs such as \i OS/8 PAL8\i0  and \i P?S/8 PAL\i0 .  In\par
certain cases, the output can be tainted by the deliberate insertion of\par
inappropriate characters as part of an ill-conceived design (which was demanded by\par
programming managers with a twisted agenda).  The reasoning behind all of these\par
issues (or lack thereof) is beyond the scope of this document; the only concern is how the STRIP.TEC program resolves them.\par
\par
Note: For more information about the problems addressed above, contact the author using the indicated email address; include in the subject line: "Typeset-8 Edict".\page The PEPS package enhancements allow easy correction of lineprinter (and simulated paper-tape et al.) text files.  For those interested in viewing specific quirks, the files can be displayed before and after processing by the STRIP.TEC program.\par
\par
Note: Viewing \i OS/8\i0 -generated text files prior to processing by STRIP.TEC may be problematic; the files are often created with mark parity set on every character.  Windows\super tm\nosupersub -based editors are available within the PEPS package (such as \i EditPad\i0  \i Lite\super tm\nosupersub\i0 ), which can interpret text encoding as seven-bit ASCII using custom program settings.\par
\par
\b TECO macro arguments and execution.\b0\par
\par
The TECO macro STRIP.TEC is launched by the following command contained within certain PEPS package batch files; specific command usage is initiated by use of a shortcut in the \ul Commands\ulnone  section of the package.  Within each relevant batch file,  command-line usage is as follows:\par
\par
\ul TECOC MUNG STRIP.TEC,%STRIPFILE%\ulnone\par
\par
The \i STRIPFILE\i0  environment variable contents are derived from the \ul %1\ulnone  argument as passed to the relevant batch file.  By specifying an independent variable, flexibility in command formation is achieved and readability is enhanced.\par
\par
Note: Generally, the contents of STRIPFILE will be the complete pathname of the text file to be processed.\par
\par
The \i MUNG\i0  sub-command places the entire contents of STRIP.TEC into an internal buffer within TECOC; this is the usual mechanism for passing TECO macro contents into TECOC.  Other TECO implementations (such as the \i OS/8\i0  version) use a separate MUNG command as an analogous TECO macro startup mechanisn.\par
\par
Note: Various implementations of TECO have analogous startup mechanisms (perhaps) incompatible with TECOC; the particulars, although likely minor, must be reconciled as necessary.  STRIP.TEC is designed to work only with TECOC; modifications may be necessary to use STRIP.TEC with other TECO implementations (if at all possible).\par
\par
As implemented in TECOC, the contents of the STRIPFILE environment variable are placed into the edit buffer (usually associated with text while performing conventional editing).  The execution of the STRIP.TEC macro starts with the entire macro contents read into a separate internal buffer (which can be likened to an internal unnamed \i Q-register\i0 , as text storage is referred to within TECO).  Since the STRIP.TEC macro is designed to take the pathname of the passed file as an argument, it will process the text as necessary to create dynamic TECO read or write commands (targeting the passed file for editing purposes) as required.  \par
\par
Note: Startup conventions and exact mechanisms used to pass command-line arguments to TECO are extremely implementation-specific.  In some versions, the string is passed as the text within a Q-register, perhaps different from another Q-register within which are the macro contents; see specific TECO release documentation for more information regarding startup and initial argument passing considerations.\par
 \par
Except where otherwise indicated, \ul $\ulnone  refers to the escape character (<ESC>) (the ASCII character code for <ESC> is 27 decimal, 32 octal), which has no defined printing character.  When viewing a macro program within most versions of TECO, \ul $\ulnone  characters are displayed as a substitution for <ESC>; this includes displaying <ESC> within the edit buffer using any of the usual viewing commands.  This is why it is difficult to edit TECO macros other than within TECO itself.  <ESC> is used as a terminator of a string or command in most instances; a macro must contain multiple <ESC> characters by nature.  The situation is often complicated as TECO macros are known to dynamically create other TECO macros to be executed from the originating TECO macro program.  As such, these created TECO macros will also contain multiple <ESC> characters.\par
\par
Fortunately, TECO allows direct insertion of individual characters by character\par
code.  The instruction sequence \ul 27I$\ulnone  can be used for the purpose of inserting <ESC> into the edit buffer, as the numerical value of the ASCII code for <ESC> is 27.\page Note: Some programmers are more comfortable using octal values; by executing the \ul Control-O\ulnone  command, all further numeric input will be evaluated in octal radix until a \ul Control-D\ulnone  command is executed to return the input radix to decimal.  In that context, the instruction sequence \ul 32I$\ulnone  is often used to achieve the identical result.  Future releases of STRIP.TEC may use octal radix notation given the number of special case character codes processed.\par
\par
\b Similarity of different commands.\b0\par
\par
The \ul I\ulnone nsert command, when passed a numerical argument (and terminated by an <ESC> character), creates a single character in the edit buffer with the ASCII code of the numerical argument.  This numerical form of the \ul I\ulnone  command requires a trailing escape character to distinguish this from the textual form of the \ul I\ulnone  command; when used for text insertion, an optional leading numerical argument (potentially) allows multiple insertions of the passed text string.  In textual usage, the entire string that follows is the argument, which must be terminated by an <ESC> character; <CR> and <LF> may be present in the text string for the purpose of inserting line breaks in the edit buffer.\par
\par
Note: Specifics of the textual form of the \ul I\ulnone  command might be version-dependent. STRIP.TEC avoids command extensions where possible.  The text form of the \ul I\ulnone  command inserts the entirety of an indefinite text string into the edit buffer when the terminating <ESC> is reached; however, an alternate usage of the same command could merely insert a single text character (as opposed to an evaluated character).\par
\par
Other sets of TECO commands have an analogous duality where function is determined by exact usage.  In some instances, the contents of TECO macros must be carefully studied to determine the exact form of the command being used.\par
\par
\b Known Limitations.\b0\par
\par
Due to the way OS/8 creates quirky output with the standard OS/8 \i PTP:\i0  handler, it is possible for trailing <FF> characters to occasionally still be present in the file after character stripping has been performed.  As necessary, the stripping process can be repeated to remove trailing <FF> characters.  Since a recent update was made to the STRIP.TEC macro, this problem has no longer been observed.  Please report any problems with the latest release to the author.\par
\par
Additional modifications have been made to the STRIP.TEC macro to protect against malformed text files that do not comply with standard line termination rules.  Please report any specific problems to the author for possible remedy; it may be necessary to apply manual text exiting prior to character stripping in the case of exceptional character string situations not currently handled.  A possible alternative solution would be to avoid the PTP: handler and instead using the LPT: handler (which is known to exhibit fewer problems).\par
\par
Note: The quirky behavior of the standard OS/8 PTP: handler is completely by design.  For more information, contact the author using the indicated email address; include in the subject line: "Typeset-8 Edict".\par
