\par
N^X$;\tab\tab\tab    A \ul N\ulnone onstop search is performed; all characters processed will\par
                     be forced into the output buffer and (eventually) written to\par
                     the output file.  In this case, the search is a special-case\par
                     operation indicated by the single character \ul Control-X\ulnone .  This\par
                     means the search argument is any (existent) single character\par
                     of any value.  The only way the search can fail is when the\par
                     search returns a special value indicating there is no further\par
                     input to process.\page                      Note: This condition can be detected by alternate means; the\par
                     value of all "additional" characters is always set to return\par
                     \ul -1\ulnone  at the end of the input file.  The \ul Control-X\ulnone  method is used\par
                     within the STRIP.TEC program because implementation is more\par
                     straight-forward, albeit requiring a fully compliant TECO\par
                     implementation.\par
\par
                     As implemented, this mechanism is not searchable in the\par
                     ordinary sense.  It is merely the special status of the buffer\par
                     pointer being positioned at the end of the input file and not\par
                     an actual character; searching for any input character failed\par
                     to obtain a matching result. \par
\par
\tab\tab\tab    When any form of search inside of iteration brackets fails\par
\tab\tab\tab    and a trailing \ul ;\ulnone  character exists, the normal error abort\par
\tab\tab\tab    is inhibited.  The iteration loop ends and execution\par
\tab\tab\tab    continues past the \ul >\ulnone  character matching the \ul <\ulnone  character in\par
