                     applies to properly adjusted hardware; it may have merely been\par
                     used as a defensive measure applied by an ignorant programmer\par
                     not aware of various maintenance issues of the PC04/PC05\par
                     reader/punch devices.  This was especially true in the case of\par
                     DEC in-house development systems known to have had shoddy\par
                     maintenance procedures.  In essence, program development was\par
                     often performed on aberrant hardware inconsistent with user\par
                     systems nominally configured with the same devices.\page                      In the \i PEPS/SIMH\i0  environment, the notion of a malformed\par
                     character does not apply; as such, it is necessary to obtain\par
                     every character.  Unfortunately, the handler design is such\par
                     that a double-buffered approach is taken that will predictably\par
                     ignore the final character.\line\line                      To remedy this problem, \i OS/8 PIP\i0  may be used to restore the\par
                     final character (generally a <LF>) or be certain the OS/8\par
                     program that will process the file ignores <LF> characters.\par
                     (\i OS/8 PAL8\i0  delimits source lines with <CR> and ignores <LF>.)\par
\par
                     When using \i P?S/8 OS8CON\i0  to convert OS/8 input to P?S/8 output\par
                     files, this case is properly handled; when converting P?S/8\par
                     text files to OS/8 text files, the result is always properly\par
                     formed with <CR> and <LF> on every line and a Control-Z\par
                     character is always provided as a sentinel character to\par
                     signify proper end-of-file.\par
\par
                     The OS/8 high-speed punch handler situation is far worse:  A\par
                     mis-feature intended only for the Typeset-8 group was foisted\par
                     on the general OS/8 user community despite it having no\par
                     constructive purpose.\par
\par
                     The mis-feature is the deliberate insertion of otherwise\par
                     extraneous characters to attempt to format long paper tapes to\par
                     be placed into the reader stations of offline Teletype Model\par
                     ASR-35 terminals; These ASR-35 terminals were generously\par
                     available in all in-house Typeset-8 installations.  A sensible\par
                     arrangement would have included a Typeset-8-based (or other\par
                     utility program) to properly handle the particulars of this\par
                     terminal as required; instead, the paper tape is greatly\par
                     lengthened because of badly implemented attempts to directly\par
                     format and print the output.\par
\par
                     While this conceptual approach is quite bad, the actual\par
                     implementation is even worse: Proper handling of the <HT>\par
                     character on this device depends on exact carriage position.\par
                     Depending on the particulars, one or perhaps two fill\par
                     characters must be placed into the data stream, allowing the\par
                     file to be self-contained (as apparently was intended).  A\par
                     simple formula \b should\b0  have been used to determine the proper\par
                     fill character count; instead a single <NUL> (or perhaps <RO>)\par
                     character is always inserted just past the <HT> character.\par
                     While this method does work in nearly half of the applicable\par
                     cases that apply, this causes the output to be needlessly\par
                     slowed down where the fill character is not required, and when\par
                     two fill characters are needed, it is inadequate, leading to\par
                     character smear on the terminal paper (as the carriage is\par
                     still in motion when the character is printed).\par
\par
                     Various filler character strategies (both adequate and\par
                     inadequate) are used for the supported control characters,\par
                     which includes <FF>, <VT> and <HT>.  Quite pointlessly,\par
                     distinct fill characters are used in some cases different from\par
                     others.  The approach used is sometimes simple-minded; the\par
                     worst-case quantity of fill characters is used instead of\par
                     using a programmed method that keeps track of where on the\par
                     printed page the current carriage position would be located.\par
                     (Had that better method been used, the typical count of fill\par
                     characters would be far less.)\par
